Q3. While developing a menu-driven console application, why are loops
    preferred over conditional repetition? Explain which loop you would
    choose and why?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER:

WHY LOOPS ARE PREFERRED:

  -> In a menu-driven application, the menu must be shown REPEATEDLY until
     the user chooses to exit.
  -> Using only if-else conditions, you would have to duplicate the menu
     display code many times, which is inefficient and hard to maintain.
  -> Loops allow the menu to repeat automatically without code duplication.

PROBLEMS WITH CONDITIONAL REPETITION (without loops):
  -> Code becomes very long and repetitive.
  -> Hard to maintain -- changing the menu means editing many places.
  -> Not scalable.

WHICH LOOP TO CHOOSE -- do-while loop:

  REASON:
  -> A do-while loop executes the body AT LEAST ONCE before checking
     the condition.
  -> This is perfect for a menu because:
     * The menu should always display ONCE before asking the user's choice.
     * The loop continues until the user selects "Exit".
  -> A while loop might skip showing the menu if the condition fails first.
  -> A for loop is better when the number of repetitions is known in advance
     (not suitable here since we don't know how many times the user will use
     the menu).

EXAMPLE:

  do {
      printf("\n===== MENU =====\n");
      printf("1. Add Record\n");
      printf("2. View Records\n");
      printf("3. Delete Record\n");
      printf("4. Exit\n");
      printf("Enter your choice: ");
      scanf("%d", &choice);

      switch(choice) {
          case 1: addRecord();    break;
          case 2: viewRecords();  break;
          case 3: deleteRecord(); break;
          case 4: printf("Goodbye!\n"); break;
          default: printf("Invalid choice! Try again.\n");
      }
  } while (choice != 4);

CONCLUSION: do-while loop is the best choice for menu-driven applications
because it guarantees at least one menu display and repeats until exit.


